{"name":"Back to Nature","hex":"#bdb98f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"back to nature","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Turning away from urban environs and attitudes, towards an organic, agrarian lifestyle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/back_to_nature"}],"entries":[{"title":"Back to Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Back to Nature is a 1936 American comedy film directed by James Tinling and starring Jed Prouty, Shirley Deane and Dixie Dunbar. It is part of the Jones Family series of films."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e2/Back_to_Nature_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_to_Nature","wikidata":"Q23767808"},{"title":"Back to nature","blocks":[{"text":"Back to nature or return to nature is a philosophy or style of living which emphasises closeness to nature, rather than artifice and civilisation. In this, the rustic customs and pastoralism of country life are preferred to urban fashion and sophistication."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_to_nature","wikidata":"Q65073016"},{"title":"Human back","blocks":[{"text":"The human back, also called the dorsum, is the large posterior area of the human body, rising from the top of the buttocks to the back of the neck. It is the surface of the body opposite from the chest and the abdomen. The vertebral column runs the length of the back and creates a central area of recession. The breadth of the back is created by the shoulders at the top and the pelvis at the bottom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_back","wikidata":"Q133279"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/250px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray204.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/250px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABack_Muscles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gray798-back_crop.png/120px-Gray798-back_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th